/target

# Ignored in the repository rather than in a developer's global config, so
# the rule is the same for everyone who clones. An ignore that lives in
# ~/.config/git/ignore is invisible from here: this directory read as
# "ignored, nothing to see" on the machine it was written on and as an
# untracked file to anyone else, which makes any "nothing is untracked"
# audit true only locally. `git check-ignore -v <path>` prints the rule that
# decided, and `-c core.excludesFile=/dev/null` shows what a stranger sees.
.vscode/
